WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:09.900
You know, usually when we evaluate whether a physical structure is safe, there's this built-in expectation of simple, almost primitive counting.

00:10.260 --> 00:12.120
Right, yeah, like taking an inventory.

00:12.340 --> 00:15.060
Exactly. Think of a Jenga tower sitting on a table.

00:15.320 --> 00:23.580
If you want to know if it's a complete set, you look at it, you count the individual wooden blocks, and your mental ledger says, okay, we have exactly 54 blocks.

00:23.640 --> 00:23.880
Right.

00:23.880 --> 00:29.200
The inventory is full, the stock is complete, therefore the structure is perfectly safe.

00:29.200 --> 00:30.960
You just check the box and walk away.

00:31.460 --> 00:34.100
It's an incredibly comforting way to view the world, really.

00:34.840 --> 00:37.480
I mean, we inherently like things to be visible.

00:37.660 --> 00:38.180
Oh, for sure.

00:38.260 --> 00:42.660
We're able to be strictly categorized, to sit neatly onto a spreadsheet line item.

00:43.300 --> 00:46.920
The blocks are either sitting there in physical space or they aren't.

00:47.080 --> 00:49.100
It's a completely binary state of existence.

00:49.380 --> 00:58.720
Right, but then you step into the world of complex living ecosystems, and suddenly that simple counting method is, well, it's not just inadequate, it is structurally broken.

00:58.720 --> 01:00.160
Yeah, completely broke it.

01:00.240 --> 01:10.860
It's the equivalent of evaluating that exact same Jenga tower, just by standing over it and counting the 54 blocks, but totally ignoring the actual physical geometry of the tower itself.

01:11.000 --> 01:13.140
Right, ignoring how it's actually built.

01:13.240 --> 01:13.400
Yeah.

01:13.840 --> 01:24.920
You ignore the fact that all the load-bearing bottom blocks have been carefully pulled out, the entire structure is swaying in the breeze, and to top it all off, someone is actively kicking the legs of the table.

01:25.120 --> 01:25.980
That's a great visual.

01:25.980 --> 01:29.040
The 54 blocks might technically still be there today.

01:29.380 --> 01:34.440
I mean, your ledger still balances perfectly, but the collapse is already a physical guarantee.

01:34.840 --> 01:38.700
The system is doomed, even while the inventory looks flawless.

01:39.080 --> 01:43.820
And that scenario you just described is the absolute definition of a structural blind spot.

01:43.820 --> 01:56.060
But when you scale that specific blind spot up, when you apply that exact same flawed counting mechanism to the entire global carbon offset market, the implications aren't just an accounting error.

01:56.320 --> 01:56.620
Right.

01:56.680 --> 01:58.480
They are staggering on a planetary scale.

01:58.640 --> 02:02.820
And that massive planetary scale blind spot is exactly what we are getting into today.

02:03.220 --> 02:04.520
Welcome to The Deep Dive.

02:04.560 --> 02:05.160
Glad to be here.

02:05.160 --> 02:17.760
We are thrilled you're here with us because today we are unpacking a remarkably dense, highly theoretical, and I would argue profoundly important, 2026 paper by independent researcher Flixchen.

02:17.940 --> 02:19.940
Yeah, it's called Proxy Permanence Failure.

02:20.060 --> 02:20.360
Right.

02:20.360 --> 02:27.840
And our mission for you in this deep dive is to explore the absolute foundation of the modern carbon offset market.

02:28.140 --> 02:38.220
If you think about it, from protecting ancient terrestrial forests to preserving coastal blue carbon like kelp and seagrass.

02:38.320 --> 02:40.460
The entire multi-billion dollar system, really.

02:40.460 --> 02:40.820
Yes.

02:40.960 --> 02:48.120
The whole thing is built on a very simple premise, that carbon is a stable, tradable stock, like gold in a vault.

02:48.440 --> 02:48.880
Exactly.

02:49.260 --> 03:02.120
Today we're going to explore why this assumption is dangerously false, how our rigid accounting systems are literally collapsing under the weight of climate change, and what a radically new, unified mathematical framework suggests we do to fix it.

03:02.220 --> 03:07.280
It really is a phenomenal piece of literature because Flixchen doesn't just point out a flaw in the market and complain about it.

03:07.300 --> 03:07.500
Right.

03:07.540 --> 03:08.520
It's not just a takedown.

03:08.760 --> 03:09.580
No, not at all.

03:09.580 --> 03:19.580
The paper diagnoses a foundational, almost philosophical mismatch in how human beings attempt to measure and govern the natural world.

03:19.820 --> 03:26.160
Okay, so let's unpack this because I want to set the stage right away so we don't get the wrong idea about, you know, the villain of the story.

03:26.360 --> 03:27.040
Yeah, the disorder.

03:27.240 --> 03:34.040
Flixchen is not framing this as some malicious mustache twirling scam by greedy corporations.

03:34.480 --> 03:37.880
It's not a story about bad actors cooking the books in a back room.

03:37.880 --> 03:40.800
Right, it's more of a, well, a mathematical failure of translation.

03:40.980 --> 03:41.420
Exactly.

03:41.800 --> 03:45.220
It's a fascinating, somewhat terrifying mathematical failure.

03:46.120 --> 03:55.760
The paper talks about taking object ontology, which is just a fancy way of saying spreadsheets, static bank accounts, ledgers, stationary accounts of physical objects.

03:55.840 --> 03:56.840
The 54 Jenga blocks?

03:56.840 --> 03:58.040
The Jenga blocks, exactly.

03:58.040 --> 04:09.100
Taking that and forcefully applying it to a field ontology, meaning living, breathing, constantly shifting, hypercomplex ecosystems that fundamentally refuse to sit still for the camera.

04:09.100 --> 04:15.840
That distinction right there, object versus field ontology, that's the skeleton key that unlocks this entire paper.

04:16.160 --> 04:24.280
To truly understand why this translation failure is happening, we have to look at how human institutions simplify the chaos of the world.

04:25.000 --> 04:31.500
In the early sections, Flixchen discusses the critical difference between epistemic compression and causal compression.

04:31.680 --> 04:32.680
Okay, let's break those down.

04:32.680 --> 04:40.740
Sure, so epistemic compression is totally normal. It's necessary. If you want to understand any complex system, you have to compress the information.

04:40.940 --> 04:47.780
Right, because if I'm trying to figure out if my living room is warm enough, I just look at the thermostat. It's a 72 degrees. That's epistemic compression.

04:47.940 --> 04:48.680
Perfect example.

04:48.680 --> 04:58.560
I don't need my smart home to give me a real-time, three-dimensional map of the exact velocity and vector of every single oxygen and nitrogen molecule bouncing around the room.

04:58.760 --> 05:00.280
Your brain couldn't process it anyway.

05:00.460 --> 05:04.940
No. I drop the variables that don't fundamentally change the outcome I care about.

05:05.020 --> 05:15.480
That's a great way to look at it. You reduce millions of chaotic data points into a workable summary so that a human brain or a bureaucratic governance protocol can actually operate.

05:15.620 --> 05:16.120
Makes sense.

05:16.120 --> 05:24.340
But, and this is where Flixchen drops the hammer, what current carbon governance models are doing is not epistemic compression.

05:24.660 --> 05:25.040
Oh.

05:25.820 --> 05:39.540
They're engaging in causal compression. They haven't just dropped the background noise. They have actively stripped away the exact vital variables that causally govern whether the ecosystem survives in the first place.

05:39.740 --> 05:45.780
So they aren't just ignoring the velocity of the air molecules. They are ignoring the fact that the house is on fire.

05:45.780 --> 05:48.940
Or that the walls holding the roof up are rotting from the inside.

05:49.080 --> 05:49.220
Yeah.

05:49.400 --> 05:52.820
They've removed the architecture that actually keeps a forest alive.

05:52.940 --> 05:53.340
Oh.

05:53.860 --> 06:01.120
They ignore biodiversity. They ignore the complex interactions of disturbances like fire and drought. They ignore nutrient flows.

06:01.240 --> 06:02.340
They just compress it all down.

06:02.340 --> 06:14.140
They compress the entire living ecosystem down to a single dead number tons of carbon and assume that number is physically detached from the fragile living web that actually holds it in place.

06:14.220 --> 06:22.280
Which brings us right back to my Jenga tower. The market ledger is just counting the raw volume of wood. It is completely blind to the stability of the structure.

06:22.280 --> 06:35.860
So if the ledger is fundamentally wrong, if our global carbon accounting system is essentially living in a mathematical fantasy world, what does the real world collapse actually look like?

06:35.940 --> 06:36.980
Well, it's pretty severe.

06:37.220 --> 06:44.320
Because one thing I loved about this paper is that Fliction doesn't just sit in an ivory tower and theorize. They bring receipts.

06:44.320 --> 06:53.720
Oh, they bring devastating empirical receipts. Fliction synthesizes three very distinct, highly respected empirical studies, all published very recently.

06:53.840 --> 06:55.260
Right. The three failure modes.

06:55.340 --> 07:00.620
Yes. They expose three completely different failure modes in our nature-based climate solutions.

07:01.160 --> 07:07.480
And what is so brilliant about the synthesis is how the paper proves these aren't isolated, unrelated incidents.

07:07.560 --> 07:08.160
They're connected.

07:08.440 --> 07:12.060
They are different symptoms of the exact same underlying mathematical disease.

07:12.060 --> 07:19.360
So let's trace this disease. Let's look at the first symptom, which the paper highlights via a 2026 study by Wu and colleagues.

07:19.500 --> 07:21.600
Ah, yes. The CARB study.

07:21.880 --> 07:27.580
Right. This focuses on the California Air Resources Board, generally known as CARB.

07:28.360 --> 07:35.960
For context for you listening, this is one of the biggest, most prestigious, and heavily scrutinized carbon cap and trade programs in the world.

07:36.040 --> 07:37.860
It sets the standard for a lot of the industry.

07:37.860 --> 07:51.240
Exactly. When you buy a forest offset through CARB, say you want to offset your company's emissions by protecting a forest in the Sierra Nevadas, CARB takes a portion of those credits you bought and puts them into a buffer pool.

07:51.640 --> 07:58.920
Right. The buffer pool is essentially a massive system-wide insurance policy because forests are physical things, right? They can be destroyed.

07:58.920 --> 08:00.040
Yeah, they burn down.

08:00.440 --> 08:04.600
Exactly. If a forest you protect it burns down, the carbon goes back into the atmosphere.

08:05.600 --> 08:13.760
To ensure the integrity of the market, CARB retires credits from this communal buffer pool to financially and mathematically make up for the laws.

08:14.100 --> 08:16.260
It's a standard actuarial mechanism.

08:16.480 --> 08:16.860
Exactly.

08:17.080 --> 08:26.100
But the mechanism is broken because Wu's team analyzed the math and found that this buffer pool is undersized by a stagzing factor of 6.3.

08:26.260 --> 08:27.040
It's unbelievable.

08:27.040 --> 08:30.720
It's not off by a few percentage points. It's off by an order of magnitude.

08:31.360 --> 08:35.940
To really internalize what a factor of 6.3 means, imagine your home insurance.

08:36.440 --> 08:36.760
Okay.

08:37.080 --> 08:45.260
If your insurance company's actuaries underestimated the likelihood of your entire neighborhood burning down by a factor of 6, they wouldn't just have a bad quarter.

08:45.620 --> 08:46.400
They'd be done.

08:46.460 --> 08:50.900
They would be fundamentally insolvent. They would be bankrupt the moment a real fire season hit.

08:51.600 --> 08:55.620
Wu's team looked at 116 compliance forest projects in the system.

08:55.620 --> 08:56.760
And what did they find?

08:57.040 --> 09:03.740
Well, they modeled them under a moderate climate emission scenario, what the scientific community calls SSP2 4.5.

09:04.500 --> 09:15.220
Under that fairly middle-of-the-road scenario, they found that climate change roughly triples the baseline probability of wildfire reversals over a hundred-year project period.

09:15.220 --> 09:19.800
And a hundred-year period is the standard baseline for permanence in these markets, right?

09:19.800 --> 09:23.600
Correct. The promise is that the carbon will remain stored for at least a century.

09:24.380 --> 09:31.680
But Wu's data showed that 41% of the currently active, enrolled projects face a greater than 50% chance of reversing.

09:31.760 --> 09:34.240
Wait, so meaning burning down or dying off?

09:34.240 --> 09:37.940
Yes. Within that time frame, think about the gravity of that.

09:37.940 --> 09:47.880
For nearly half the market, there is a worse-than-coin flip chance that the specific carbon you paid millions of dollars to protect will literally go up in smoke.

09:48.340 --> 09:56.000
Okay, wait. How does a massive regulatory body with some of the best scientists in the world miss the mark by a factor of 6?

09:56.660 --> 09:57.460
That's a good question.

09:57.700 --> 10:00.580
Are their calculators broken? What is the blind spot here?

10:00.640 --> 10:05.040
The blind spot is a deeply ingrained statistical assumption called stationarity.

10:05.400 --> 10:06.080
Stationarity, okay.

10:06.080 --> 10:12.980
When the governance models were built, the actuaries calibrated their insurance buffer based on historical fire and drought data.

10:13.460 --> 10:15.500
They looked backward to predict the future.

10:16.780 --> 10:21.980
Stationarity assumes that the underlying system generating the risks, the climate, is stable.

10:22.220 --> 10:25.820
It assumes the future will basically look like a randomized version of the past 50 years.

10:26.000 --> 10:29.980
But climate change is the exact opposite of stationary. It is an accelerator.

10:30.380 --> 10:33.440
Exactly. The ecological conditions are deeply non-stationary.

10:33.440 --> 10:39.600
The future probability distribution of a massive wildfire is completely decoupled from the historical baseline.

10:39.960 --> 10:42.520
So they're preparing for a world that doesn't exist anymore.

10:42.720 --> 10:46.960
Yes. The protocol is assuming a static, flat risk environment,

10:47.380 --> 10:54.180
while the actual physical environment is experiencing an expanding, rapidly accelerating regime of disturbance.

10:54.180 --> 10:57.860
So failure mode 1 is essentially a failure of time.

10:58.660 --> 11:01.660
We are assuming the world is static when it's actually accelerating.

11:02.180 --> 11:05.920
We are driving down the highway looking exclusively in the rearview mirror.

11:06.060 --> 11:07.260
That's a perfect analogy.

11:07.420 --> 11:08.940
But that brings up an interesting question.

11:09.520 --> 11:10.940
What if we fix the mirrors?

11:10.940 --> 11:13.940
What if we magically had perfect climate foresight?

11:14.660 --> 11:16.940
Well, even then, the paper argues we'd still fail.

11:17.080 --> 11:18.860
Right. Which leads to failure mode 2.

11:19.500 --> 11:25.820
And this one, relying on a 2024 paper by Weisskopf and colleagues, genuinely threw me for a loop.

11:26.080 --> 11:28.940
Because it focuses entirely on biological diversity.

11:29.160 --> 11:29.420
Yes.

11:29.560 --> 11:34.280
The Weisskopf paper is a masterclass in exposing the dangers of epistemic compression.

11:34.560 --> 11:35.120
How so?

11:35.120 --> 11:41.320
They found that biodiversity loss alone, just the steady decline of species richness in an ecosystem,

11:41.480 --> 11:47.700
without even factoring in a catastrophic fire or a chainsaw, could cause massive global terrestrial carbon losses.

11:47.820 --> 11:49.000
Just from losing species.

11:49.420 --> 11:50.060
Just from that.

11:50.560 --> 11:55.760
We are talking anywhere from 7 to 146 petagrams of carbon being released.

11:55.980 --> 11:59.660
Just so we have a sense of scale, a petagram is a billion metric tons.

11:59.920 --> 12:03.920
It is an almost unfathomable amount of carbon escaping into the atmosphere.

12:03.920 --> 12:11.160
And what Weisskopf's team demonstrated is a catastrophic failure in how we model the very concept of vegetation.

12:11.620 --> 12:11.660
Right.

12:12.020 --> 12:17.660
When scientists try to predict global carbon cycles, they use what are called Earth System Models, or ESMs.

12:18.120 --> 12:20.920
These are incredibly complex supercomputer simulations.

12:21.580 --> 12:22.540
But they have limits.

12:22.720 --> 12:23.120
Exactly.

12:23.680 --> 12:27.980
Even a supercomputer cannot track every single blade of grass or fungal spore on Earth.

12:27.980 --> 12:34.520
So to make the math computationally viable, these models use that massive epistemic compression we talked about earlier.

12:34.660 --> 12:35.580
They simplify it.

12:35.660 --> 12:35.820
Yes.

12:35.880 --> 12:36.120
Yes.

12:36.320 --> 12:43.180
They group the staggering, infinite complexity of global vegetation into less than 15 simple categories called plant functional types.

12:43.340 --> 12:48.620
So instead of a model recognizing a million unique species interacting in a jungle, the model just says,

12:48.720 --> 12:53.400
okay, this pixel is a broadleaf tree, this pixel is a needleleaf tree, this pixel is a shrub.

12:53.480 --> 12:53.860
Exactly.

12:53.860 --> 12:57.960
It turns a rainforest into a cartoon drawing of a forest.

12:58.140 --> 12:59.020
That's a perfect visual.

12:59.560 --> 13:07.560
And by reducing the forest to a cartoon, the models become entirely blind to a very real, empirically proven relationship in ecology.

13:07.840 --> 13:08.060
Yeah.

13:08.400 --> 13:12.920
Biomass is proportional to species richness to the power of 0.26.

13:13.300 --> 13:13.540
Okay.

13:13.660 --> 13:19.440
I have to stop you there because whenever a deep dive drops an exponent, my eyes glaze over and I know I'm not alone.

13:19.720 --> 13:20.160
Fair enough.

13:20.160 --> 13:21.340
Let's translate this.

13:21.980 --> 13:27.020
What does an exponent of 0.26 actually look like if I am standing in the middle of a forest?

13:27.020 --> 13:30.320
What is the physical mechanism happening around me?

13:30.840 --> 13:33.820
Think of it as a mathematically guaranteed penalty for simplicity.

13:34.600 --> 13:40.900
In a highly diverse forest one with hundreds of different species, you have what ecologists call niche partitioning.

13:41.020 --> 13:42.080
Niche partitioning.

13:42.140 --> 13:42.300
Yeah.

13:42.660 --> 13:48.620
You have different species of trees whose roots reach entirely different depth in the soil, drawing water from different aquifers.

13:48.620 --> 13:53.020
You have different canopy structures that utilize completely different wavelengths of sunlight.

13:53.440 --> 13:54.040
Ah, okay.

13:54.080 --> 13:58.180
You have specific fungal networks that trade nutrients with specific root systems.

13:58.360 --> 13:59.580
They cover for each other.

13:59.780 --> 14:03.080
They optimize every square inch of available resources.

14:03.080 --> 14:09.200
So they are ultra-efficient because they aren't all competing for the exact same drop of water or ray of light.

14:09.540 --> 14:09.980
Precisely.

14:10.540 --> 14:24.620
Now, if you start losing species, even if the remaining trees look perfectly healthy to a passing observer, the ecosystem's fundamental mechanical capacity to produce and maintain total biomass drops.

14:24.840 --> 14:26.080
It's like an engine losing parts.

14:26.080 --> 14:29.060
The machinery loses its specialized parts, exactly.

14:29.580 --> 14:35.200
Because our Earth system models ignore this species' richness, they completely miss this mechanism.

14:35.300 --> 14:36.760
They assume a tree is a tree is a tree.

14:36.820 --> 14:37.020
Right.

14:37.480 --> 14:45.920
But the math of the 0.26 power law dictates that less diversity equals a direct, inevitable physical penalty to carbon storage.

14:46.300 --> 14:49.960
The carbon literally bleeds out of the system as the biodiversity drops.

14:50.120 --> 14:50.440
Wow.

14:50.440 --> 14:53.440
So failure mode 1 was ignoring the acceleration of the climate.

14:53.960 --> 14:58.040
Failure mode 2 is ignoring the biological complexity that actually holds the carbon in place.

14:58.160 --> 14:58.440
Right.

14:58.620 --> 15:00.120
Which brings us to failure mode 3.

15:00.280 --> 15:04.640
And I'm going to push back on this one because when I read this section in the paper, it felt wildly counterintuitive.

15:04.780 --> 15:06.560
Oh, the marine DOC study.

15:06.840 --> 15:07.180
Yes.

15:07.980 --> 15:09.520
It didn't make physical sense to me.

15:10.500 --> 15:16.600
This relies on a 2026 study by Wananabe and colleagues concerning marine blue carbon.

15:16.600 --> 15:23.000
Specifically, they were looking at kelp, macroalgae, and seagrasses in coastal Japan.

15:23.340 --> 15:26.400
Blue carbon has become the new darling of the offset markets.

15:26.740 --> 15:26.940
Yeah.

15:27.020 --> 15:32.980
Because coastal ecosystems can draw down massive amounts of carbon, often much faster than terrestrial forests.

15:33.300 --> 15:33.580
Right.

15:33.700 --> 15:36.380
But the carbon they draw down doesn't just stay in the plant.

15:36.720 --> 15:40.440
A lot of it gets exported as dissolved organic carbon, or DOC.

15:40.540 --> 15:42.700
It literally just floats out into the open ocean.

15:42.760 --> 15:43.140
Exactly.

15:43.140 --> 15:51.960
Now, Watanabe found that a massive chunk of this DOC, between 14 and 25 percent over a 100-year timescale, is what they call recalcitrant.

15:52.360 --> 15:53.620
Meaning, it doesn't degrade.

15:53.840 --> 15:55.820
It just stays locked up in the water column.

15:55.880 --> 15:56.060
Yeah.

15:56.260 --> 16:01.020
And this represents 4 to 8 percent of the plant's total annual net primary production.

16:01.520 --> 16:02.820
But here is where I struggled.

16:02.920 --> 16:03.180
Okay.

16:03.580 --> 16:09.200
You're telling me that this carbon is just floating freely in the ocean, and it isn't locked in some physical vault.

16:09.320 --> 16:10.600
It isn't buried in mud.

16:10.600 --> 16:13.300
It's just sitting there in the water, but nothing can eat it.

16:13.740 --> 16:14.760
That's what the data shows.

16:15.080 --> 16:17.880
But bacteria are everywhere in the ocean.

16:18.260 --> 16:23.620
How does that carbon not get consumed and turned back into CO2?

16:23.780 --> 16:24.660
It's a great question.

16:25.020 --> 16:28.580
And the answer requires a complete shift in how we think about chemistry.

16:29.040 --> 16:35.000
Usually when we think about something surviving in the ocean for a century, we assume it has some inherent chemical armor.

16:35.140 --> 16:36.580
Like it's toxic or something.

16:36.580 --> 16:39.560
Or just chemically inert, like a microscopic piece of plastic.

16:39.740 --> 16:39.900
Yeah.

16:39.900 --> 16:42.980
But Watanabe's team proved that it's absolutely false.

16:43.380 --> 16:48.460
They took this recalcitrant carbon into the lab, and they ran aggressive degradation experiments.

16:48.640 --> 16:48.780
Okay.

16:48.880 --> 16:49.640
They added sunlight.

16:49.840 --> 16:50.880
They added missing nutrients.

16:51.300 --> 16:58.340
They even inoculated the water with entirely new communities of ravenous microbes to try and force the carbon to break down.

16:58.400 --> 16:59.340
And it still survived.

16:59.340 --> 17:00.260
It didn't just survive.

17:00.340 --> 17:01.440
It barely degraded at all.

17:01.540 --> 17:01.680
Yeah.

17:01.800 --> 17:05.960
Because the carbon's persistence isn't an intrinsic property of the molecule itself.

17:06.120 --> 17:12.540
It persists due to a relational mechanism the paper calls transformation accessibility contraction.

17:12.700 --> 17:12.860
Okay.

17:12.920 --> 17:13.960
That is a mouthful.

17:14.080 --> 17:15.600
What does that mean in terms of the microbes?

17:15.960 --> 17:26.340
To put it simply, the local microbes literally run out of the specific chemical keys required to unlock and degrade those specific highly complex carbon molecules.

17:27.120 --> 17:27.400
Keys.

17:27.400 --> 17:27.640
Keys.

17:27.740 --> 17:27.880
Yeah.

17:28.500 --> 17:36.760
When kelp releases DOC, the microbes quickly swarm and eat the easy carbon, the simple sugars, the stuff that takes very little energy to break apart.

17:36.840 --> 17:37.500
The fast food.

17:37.660 --> 17:37.920
Right.

17:38.580 --> 17:46.520
As that easy stuff is consumed, what is left behind begins to spontaneously reorganize into humic-like configurations.

17:47.280 --> 17:49.700
These structures are insanely complex.

17:49.800 --> 17:50.040
Okay.

17:50.040 --> 17:57.940
To break them down, a microbe would have to expend more energy producing specialized enzymes than it would actually gain from eating the carbon.

17:58.020 --> 17:58.380
Oh.

17:58.740 --> 18:00.180
So it's not that they can't eat it.

18:00.260 --> 18:03.280
It's that it makes zero economic sense for the microbe to try.

18:03.400 --> 18:03.860
Exactly.

18:04.200 --> 18:06.840
It's what Flixson calls an operational inadmissibility.

18:06.840 --> 18:18.000
The carbon could theoretically be degraded in a lab if you applied massive chemical force, but in the actual operational thermodynamic reality of that specific ocean environment, it is completely inaccessible.

18:18.000 --> 18:23.660
That is wild. It's like leaving a massive locked safe full of food in the middle of a hungry crowd.

18:23.780 --> 18:24.580
That's exactly it.

18:24.960 --> 18:28.100
The food inside isn't indestructible. It's totally edible.

18:28.100 --> 18:35.260
But nobody in the crowd has the combination to the safe, and nobody has the tools to crack it, so the food just sits there forever.

18:36.060 --> 18:41.040
The carbon's permanence is defined by the lack of capability of everything around it.

18:41.160 --> 18:44.300
And this is the fatal flaw for the blue carbon offset markets.

18:45.080 --> 18:49.100
Current accounting frameworks do not measure this microbial accessibility at all.

18:49.180 --> 18:50.100
They just measure the stock.

18:50.220 --> 18:51.980
They measure the carbon as a flat stock.

18:52.420 --> 18:53.660
They assume it's a binary state.

18:53.760 --> 18:56.300
Either the carbon is physically present or it's gone.

18:56.300 --> 19:06.620
They completely miss the geometric relational reality that the carbon is only persisting because the biological pathways to degrade it have temporarily contracted.

19:06.900 --> 19:07.040
Wow.

19:07.220 --> 19:21.120
If the microbial community changes or if the ocean temperature shifts and alters the thermodynamic math for those microbes, that permanent carbon could suddenly become an all-you-can-eat buffet and all-it-stored CO2 rushes right back into the atmosphere.

19:21.420 --> 19:25.180
Think about what this actually does to your worldview if you're listening to this right now.

19:25.180 --> 19:33.380
You go online, you buy a certified carbon credit to offset a long-haul flight, you naturally imagine you've bought a physical guarantee.

19:33.540 --> 19:33.700
Right.

19:33.840 --> 19:38.420
A literal ton of carbon locked in a pine tree or floating safely in a kelp forest.

19:38.720 --> 19:44.180
But what Fliction is showing us through these three studies is that your credit is merely a statistical illusion.

19:44.960 --> 19:48.280
It's a projection based on outdated historical fire data.

19:48.280 --> 19:51.640
It actively ignores the biodiversity that keeps trees alive.

19:51.860 --> 19:54.940
And it completely misunderstands the fluid chemistry of the ocean.

19:55.340 --> 19:58.120
And this is where the genius of the paper synthesis shines.

19:58.800 --> 20:06.740
Because how do you connect wildfires in California, global biodiversity loss in supercomputer models, and dissolved algae off the coast of Japan?

20:06.900 --> 20:07.080
Right.

20:07.140 --> 20:08.340
They seem totally unrelated.

20:08.720 --> 20:11.300
But Fliction argues they are not three separate problems.

20:11.720 --> 20:14.280
They all represent the exact same underlying disease.

20:14.280 --> 20:19.140
Which brings us to the core concept of the paper, proxy permanence failure.

20:19.580 --> 20:27.440
Proxy permanence failure is defined as the progressive decoupling of a symbolic accounting projection from its causal ecological substrate.

20:27.820 --> 20:30.800
Meaning the symbol and the reality drift apart.

20:30.800 --> 20:44.600
The carbon credit sitting comfortably on a corporate ledger retains its perceived financial value, while the physical living reality it is supposed to represent quietly degrades, shifts, and collapses.

20:44.820 --> 20:44.980
Okay.

20:45.180 --> 20:55.680
So if the old way of measuring counting the blocks in the Jenga tower using object ontology is fundamentally mathematically broken across every ecosystem, what is the alternative?

20:55.960 --> 20:57.020
We have to measure it differently.

20:57.260 --> 21:00.040
How does Fliction propose we actually measure reality?

21:00.040 --> 21:03.620
Because we can't just throw our hands up and stop trying to fund conservation.

21:03.880 --> 21:05.100
No, we definitely can't.

21:05.300 --> 21:10.060
But to fix the problem, Fliction argues we have to change the mathematics from the ground up.

21:10.340 --> 21:12.460
We have to adopt a field ontology.

21:12.780 --> 21:13.940
A field ontology?

21:13.940 --> 21:18.000
The paper introduces what it calls the RSVP field representation.

21:19.020 --> 21:24.560
It states we must represent an ecosystem not as a scalar reservoir carbon, but as a dynamic field triple.

21:24.920 --> 21:28.160
The formulation is E of T equals 5VS.

21:28.160 --> 21:29.300
Okay, I see Greek letters.

21:29.500 --> 21:33.160
Let's translate the field triple into something I can visualize walking through the woods.

21:33.620 --> 21:35.840
E of T equals 5VS.

21:36.000 --> 21:37.120
Let's take them one by one.

21:37.380 --> 21:37.640
Sure.

21:38.020 --> 21:39.800
The first variable is phi.

21:40.480 --> 21:43.560
This represents the stored carbon and biomass density.

21:44.300 --> 21:45.860
This is everything we currently measure.

21:45.940 --> 21:46.520
So the wood.

21:46.520 --> 21:53.040
This is the solid wood in the trunks of the trees, the carbon locked in the roots, the organic matter in the topsoil.

21:53.480 --> 21:56.920
So phi is the physical stock, the stuff you can touch.

21:57.060 --> 21:57.380
Exactly.

21:57.860 --> 22:01.400
But a living ecosystem is not just a warehouse of stuff.

22:01.720 --> 22:05.280
It is also V, which stands for material flow.

22:05.560 --> 22:06.200
Flow of what?

22:06.200 --> 22:07.440
This is the movement.

22:07.980 --> 22:17.400
Nutrient cycling through the fungal networks, hydrological transport of water from the deep soil to the canopy, the way a fire or a pest infestation physically propagates across a landscape.

22:17.860 --> 22:19.880
It is the kinetic energy of the system.

22:20.100 --> 22:21.120
The stock and the flow.

22:21.220 --> 22:21.980
Okay, that makes sense.

22:22.160 --> 22:23.200
But what is the third piece?

22:23.280 --> 22:23.900
What's the S?

22:24.280 --> 22:27.140
The third piece is the most crucial, and it's the one we completely ignore.

22:27.540 --> 22:31.120
It is S, which stands for the configurational entropy field.

22:31.120 --> 22:31.760
Now, hold on.

22:31.860 --> 22:35.500
When I hear entropy, my brain immediately goes to high school physics.

22:35.920 --> 22:42.660
I think of chaos, disorder, a coffee cup shattering on the floor, the inevitable heat death of the universe.

22:43.380 --> 22:46.680
How is entropy being used here to describe a healthy forest?

22:47.440 --> 22:51.540
In this context, Flitchin uses entropy in the statistical mechanics sense.

22:52.040 --> 22:56.460
They define S as the logarithmic volume of accessible ecological futures.

22:56.700 --> 22:58.560
Volume of accessible futures.

22:58.560 --> 23:05.480
It essentially measures how many viable structural pathways an ecosystem has available to survive and recover from a shock.

23:05.800 --> 23:08.740
Give me an example of what that means for a tree or a forest.

23:08.860 --> 23:13.620
If an ecosystem has high S, it has a broad, robust ensemble of recovery trajectories.

23:14.060 --> 23:21.980
If a massive wildfire sweeps through, an ecosystem with high S has fire-resistant seeds buried in the soil waiting for the heat to trigger them.

23:22.040 --> 23:22.920
It has a backup plan.

23:23.140 --> 23:24.140
Multiple backup plans.

23:24.260 --> 23:24.280
Yeah.

23:24.280 --> 23:28.100
It has deep, protected roof structures that will immediately send up new shoots.

23:28.560 --> 23:32.240
It has a massive diversity of pioneer species ready to stabilize the ash.

23:32.480 --> 23:33.600
It has high elasticity.

23:33.920 --> 23:34.540
It has options.

23:34.660 --> 23:35.320
And low S.

23:35.760 --> 23:41.520
Conversely, if an ecosystem has low S, it is constrained to a very narrow set of paths.

23:41.980 --> 23:43.500
It has no backup plans.

23:43.900 --> 23:48.060
It is teetering on the edge of a deterministic, irreversible collapse.

23:48.060 --> 23:50.860
This right here is where the entire paper clicked for me.

23:50.940 --> 23:51.200
Really?

23:51.580 --> 23:51.920
Yes.

23:52.720 --> 24:07.020
Because measuring just the carbon in a forest, measuring just the phi and ignoring the S, is exactly like judging the health of a massive corporation solely by looking at the cash sitting in its register on a Tuesday afternoon.

24:07.660 --> 24:09.300
That's a fascinating way to look at it.

24:09.340 --> 24:11.140
You look at the cash, and it looks great.

24:11.400 --> 24:20.800
But you are completely ignoring the fact that overnight, all of their global supply chains collapsed, the CEO abruptly resigned, and their main manufacturing plant just caught on fire.

24:21.000 --> 24:21.180
Right.

24:21.280 --> 24:25.260
The physical cash in the register is the exact same today as it was yesterday.

24:25.260 --> 24:31.040
The phi hasn't changed, but the accessible futures, the S for that company, are drastically violently different.

24:31.520 --> 24:32.640
The company is dead.

24:33.020 --> 24:34.880
The ledger just hasn't realized it yet.

24:34.960 --> 24:41.640
That is a phenomenal analogy, and it perfectly illustrates why focusing only on phi is fatal for carbon markets.

24:42.040 --> 24:44.580
Let's apply your corporate analogy back to the forest.

24:44.900 --> 24:47.860
Imagine two carbon offset projects side by side.

24:48.480 --> 24:53.380
Forest A is a highly diverse, messy, chaotic, old growth ecosystem.

24:53.380 --> 24:57.980
It has massive biodiversity, complex nutrient flows, and high S.

24:58.060 --> 24:59.100
A healthy forest.

24:59.400 --> 25:07.240
Forest B is a monoculture pine plantation that a corporation planted 10 years ago specifically to harvest carbon credits.

25:07.780 --> 25:10.520
It has rows and rows of the exact same species of tree.

25:11.040 --> 25:12.680
It has incredibly low S.

25:12.840 --> 25:17.320
But today, at this exact moment, they might contain the exact same amount of total carbon.

25:17.540 --> 25:19.080
Their phi is identical.

25:19.080 --> 25:24.840
Yes, and because our current markets use object ontology, they price them identically.

25:25.260 --> 25:28.920
They issue the exact same number of carbon credits for Forest A and Forest B.

25:29.060 --> 25:30.180
That's insane.

25:30.460 --> 25:37.200
But under the non-stationary forcing of climate change, the accelerating droughts and fires, Forest A has a massive volume of recovery trajectories.

25:37.340 --> 25:37.800
It can adapt.

25:37.980 --> 25:38.800
And Forest B?

25:39.060 --> 25:47.100
Forest B, with its single species and zero backup plans, is quite literally a single drought or a single novel beetle infestation away from total erasure.

25:47.100 --> 25:52.920
The market treats them as identical static stocks, but their trajectories through time are entirely different.

25:53.240 --> 25:56.880
This represents the fundamental philosophical shift Flexion is demanding.

25:58.200 --> 26:07.820
We have to move from stock ontology treating nature like a static warehouse to trajectory ontology treating nature as a set of unfolding paths over time.

26:08.040 --> 26:12.460
You simply cannot ensure a trajectory by pretending it is a static stock.

26:12.580 --> 26:13.340
You really can't.

26:13.340 --> 26:19.440
It is genuinely amazing how deeply ingrained this stock ontology is in human thinking, though.

26:19.920 --> 26:23.000
It's how we are trained to measure everything in modern society.

26:23.120 --> 26:23.540
Oh, absolutely.

26:23.600 --> 26:24.740
It's how we measure GDP.

26:25.060 --> 26:26.700
It's how we look at our retirement accounts.

26:26.840 --> 26:28.620
It's how stores manage their inventory.

26:29.160 --> 26:34.060
Applying it to a force feels so comfortable and logical to a bureaucrat or an investment banker.

26:34.280 --> 26:34.800
It's safe.

26:34.800 --> 26:43.620
But Flexion is using heavy mathematics to prove that it is physically incoherent when you are dealing with living, breathing, reacting systems.

26:43.620 --> 26:51.200
It is mathematically incoherent because the omitted variables, the flow V and the entropy S, are not just background noise.

26:51.660 --> 26:53.620
They are what the paper calls causally constitutive.

26:54.280 --> 27:00.120
I want to pause on that phrase, causally constitutive, because a skeptic might say, look, scientists aren't stupid.

27:00.540 --> 27:04.900
Earth system models are built by some of the most brilliant climate scientists on the planet.

27:04.900 --> 27:11.180
If these entropy variables are so vital, why do our massive supercomputers ignore them?

27:11.820 --> 27:17.940
Because, mathematically, when you look at a spreadsheet, they appear numerically negligible.

27:18.120 --> 27:19.580
They just look too small to matter.

27:19.780 --> 27:19.940
Right.

27:20.220 --> 27:25.440
In a perfectly stable, stationary climate, the kind of climate we had for the last 10,000 years,

27:25.880 --> 27:30.740
you can largely get away with ignoring them because their effects are mostly perturbative.

27:31.180 --> 27:31.660
Perturbative?

27:31.660 --> 27:36.320
They just create tiny, manageable fluctuations around a stable baseline.

27:37.060 --> 27:40.920
But Flexion's core argument is that tiny does not mean unimportant.

27:41.760 --> 27:47.440
And to prove this, they make one of the most mind-bending, seemingly bizarre connections in the entire paper.

27:47.580 --> 27:49.080
Yes, the AI connection.

27:49.200 --> 27:51.380
This was the absolute highlight of the paper for me.

27:51.420 --> 27:52.040
It's so good.

27:52.120 --> 27:57.180
This was the moment I actually had to stop reading, put the paper down, and paste around the room for a minute to process it.

27:57.180 --> 28:05.820
Flexion draws a rigorous structural mathematical parallel between ecological biodiversity in a forest and the internal architecture of large language models.

28:06.040 --> 28:08.900
LLMs, the neural networks powering modern AI.

28:09.200 --> 28:09.720
Exactly.

28:10.020 --> 28:15.020
They based this on a 2026 study by Wang and colleagues about something called scale vectors.

28:15.580 --> 28:17.940
Let's dive into this because it blew my mind.

28:18.120 --> 28:19.380
It is a stunning parallel.

28:19.380 --> 28:25.520
This is where Flexion formally proves the concept of something being negligible but constitutive.

28:25.700 --> 28:26.840
Okay, walk us through it.

28:27.200 --> 28:33.940
In their study of a 1 billion parameter AI model, Wang's team was analyzing the normalization layers.

28:34.540 --> 28:44.740
Now, without getting lost in computer science, normalization layers help keep the data flowing through the AI stable so the network doesn't mathematically spiral out of control during training.

28:44.900 --> 28:45.200
Okay.

28:45.200 --> 28:48.160
Inside these layers are things called scale vectors.

28:48.820 --> 28:54.800
Wang's team found that these scale vectors make up an incredibly minuscule fraction of the model's total parameters.

28:54.940 --> 28:55.600
How minuscule?

28:56.380 --> 29:01.160
Specifically, they account for 7.84 times 10 to the negative 5 of the entire code.

29:01.360 --> 29:02.880
It is a microscopic fraction.

29:03.040 --> 29:05.060
It is a rounding error of a rounding error.

29:05.220 --> 29:05.660
Exactly.

29:06.120 --> 29:11.120
And furthermore, mathematically, Wang's team proved that these vectors are expressively redundant.

29:11.520 --> 29:12.080
Meaning what?

29:12.080 --> 29:19.700
That means you could, theoretically, absorb the linear algebra of these scale vectors into other larger parts of the neural network.

29:19.860 --> 29:20.060
Yeah.

29:20.240 --> 29:22.880
On paper, they seem completely, utterly unnecessary.

29:23.280 --> 29:25.920
But when they actually tried to remove them, what happened?

29:26.100 --> 29:27.400
The entire system collapsed.

29:27.460 --> 29:28.940
Just from that tiny fraction.

29:28.940 --> 29:35.600
When they removed those statistically negligible scale vectors, the AI's training process completely broke down.

29:36.360 --> 29:45.140
The model's token efficiency, its ability to actually generate coherent language, dropped by 1.2 to 1.4 times, and the terminal loss spiked.

29:45.360 --> 29:45.460
Wow.

29:45.460 --> 29:47.680
The model lost its internal geometry.

29:48.160 --> 29:52.540
It lost its ability to converge on the right answers and started hallucinating wildly.

29:52.740 --> 30:01.460
Okay, so we have a tiny, seemingly redundant fraction of a system that is secretly acting as the invisible glue holding the entire mass of architecture together.

30:01.900 --> 30:06.660
Now, how on earth does Fliction map the internal code of an AI to a living forest?

30:06.880 --> 30:12.320
Fliction argues beautifully that biological diversity is the scale vector of the ecosystem.

30:12.520 --> 30:13.560
Okay, I see where this is going.

30:13.560 --> 30:21.260
Think about the physical mass of a forest, the sheer biomass of the structural wood, the carbon stock, the phi is enormous.

30:21.520 --> 30:22.780
It dominates the landscape.

30:22.900 --> 30:23.620
It's the bulk of it.

30:23.800 --> 30:23.960
Right.

30:24.300 --> 30:36.460
But the biodiversity, the genetic code of the thousands of different understory plants, the rare insects, the incredibly specific mycorrhizal fungi networks, makes up a tiny fraction of the total physical mass.

30:37.020 --> 30:40.240
It's $7.84 times $10 of the forest.

30:40.500 --> 30:41.320
Conceptually, yes.

30:41.320 --> 30:44.560
If you put it on a scale, it looks statistically negligible.

30:45.260 --> 30:53.160
Our earth system models discard it because, just like the AI scale vectors, it seems way too small to matter compared to the massive bulk of the tree trunks.

30:53.400 --> 30:56.500
But just like the AI, it is dynamically constitutive.

30:56.740 --> 30:57.200
Precisely.

30:57.680 --> 31:00.740
Biodiversity acts as the constraint architecture of the ecosystem.

31:00.740 --> 31:09.080
In the AI model, the scale vectors act as a preconditioner, shaping the geometry of the learning process so the model can process chaotic data without breaking.

31:09.440 --> 31:10.240
And in the forest.

31:10.640 --> 31:15.720
In the forest, biodiversity acts as a physical preconditioner for ecological shocks.

31:15.720 --> 31:27.020
It absorbs massive disturbances, like a drought or a fire, and redistributes that kinetic energy through complex biological networks so the whole macroscopic system doesn't shatter.

31:27.020 --> 31:35.080
The paper introduces a fascinating acronym here to describe this, CLIO, Constraint Local Inference on Observables.

31:35.300 --> 31:36.360
Yes, CLIO.

31:36.540 --> 31:41.840
Now, we aren't software engineers, so how do we translate CLIO into the biological reality of a forest?

31:41.840 --> 31:44.680
What does it mean that a forest is doing inference?

31:45.220 --> 31:53.040
It means we need to stop thinking of a forest as a passive collection of wood and start recognizing it as a massively distributed biological computer.

31:53.160 --> 31:54.260
A biological computer.

31:54.440 --> 31:57.060
It is constantly performing calculations for its own survival.

31:57.480 --> 32:01.920
Every single different species encodes a distinct survival strategy, a different algorithm.

32:02.180 --> 32:02.980
Okay, give me an example.

32:03.420 --> 32:09.860
One species of oak tree knows how to survive a severe multi-year drought because of its taproot structure.

32:09.860 --> 32:15.980
A specific soil fungus knows how to chemically redistribute phosphorus when the topsoil gets too hot.

32:16.640 --> 32:22.540
A specific bird species knows how to hunt and control a sudden population boom of invasive beetles.

32:22.740 --> 32:24.560
They all hold a piece of the puzzle.

32:24.800 --> 32:26.420
They are local constraint operators.

32:26.720 --> 32:33.340
When you start losing species, even small ones, you are literally luggering the ecosystem's epistemic resolution.

32:34.080 --> 32:36.400
You are destroying its biological hardware.

32:36.600 --> 32:39.400
You are robbing it of its ability to think its way out of a crisis.

32:39.400 --> 32:44.620
I want to pause and make sure I am fully grasping the magnitude of this because it is profound.

32:45.060 --> 32:45.640
It really is.

32:45.860 --> 32:52.560
The current global carbon market looks at a monoculture pine plantation, just endless, sterile

32:52.560 --> 32:55.360
rows of the exact same genetically modified tree.

32:55.520 --> 32:59.480
And it looks at a messy, chaotic, highly diverse old growth forest.

32:59.860 --> 32:59.960
Right.

33:00.060 --> 33:04.640
It weighs the wood, sees the exact same physical amount of carbon, and issues the exact same

33:04.640 --> 33:05.780
number of carbon credits.

33:05.780 --> 33:09.000
But the pine plantation has zero scale vectors.

33:09.260 --> 33:10.960
It has no distributed inference.

33:11.200 --> 33:16.900
It is an AI model with its normalization layers violently ripped out, just sitting there

33:16.900 --> 33:23.520
in the sun, waiting to mathematically hallucinate, or in this case, dry up, invite a beetle infestation

33:23.520 --> 33:24.720
and burn to the ground.

33:24.720 --> 33:26.900
Is that essentially what Fliction is saying?

33:27.160 --> 33:28.300
You have absolutely nailed it.

33:28.400 --> 33:33.360
In fact, what you just described is the conceptual core of Fliction's admissibility volume theorem.

33:33.520 --> 33:34.380
Admissibility volume.

33:34.560 --> 33:39.720
The theorem proves mathematically that these two forces, biodiversity and climate disturbance,

33:40.260 --> 33:45.580
are locked in a dual antagonistic control over the forest's actual survival.

33:45.820 --> 33:45.980
Okay.

33:46.300 --> 33:49.680
Higher biodiversity expands the volume of resilience trajectories.

33:49.680 --> 33:52.300
It gives the forest more biological tools to survive.

33:53.100 --> 33:57.820
At the exact same time, climate-driven disturbance applies pressure that contracts that volume.

33:58.220 --> 34:01.280
And critically, these two forces do not just add or subtract.

34:01.660 --> 34:02.500
How do they interact?

34:02.660 --> 34:04.200
They compound multiplicatively.

34:04.640 --> 34:05.120
Multiplicatively.

34:05.240 --> 34:06.240
That is the scary word.

34:06.300 --> 34:07.280
They don't just add risk.

34:07.320 --> 34:08.320
They multiply the risk.

34:08.440 --> 34:08.900
Mm-hmm.

34:08.960 --> 34:09.380
Exactly.

34:09.380 --> 34:16.040
If you steadily decrease biodiversity, which human land use is doing globally while simultaneously

34:16.040 --> 34:22.420
increasing climate disturbance, the volume of accessible futures doesn't just shrink linearly.

34:22.800 --> 34:24.680
It collapses non-linearly.

34:24.840 --> 34:25.540
It falls off a cliff.

34:25.800 --> 34:30.800
The corporate carbon projection on the ledger might say the forest is 99% likely to survive

34:30.800 --> 34:35.620
the next century, but the physical reality has already contracted to the point where survival

34:35.620 --> 34:37.180
is mathematically impossible.

34:37.180 --> 34:42.180
So, we have a global system stripped of its biological defenses, the scale vectors are

34:42.180 --> 34:46.980
gone, the normalization layers are ripped out, facing an escalating, accelerating threat

34:46.980 --> 34:48.860
from non-stationary climate change.

34:48.920 --> 34:50.160
A very bad combination.

34:50.520 --> 34:55.800
If biodiversity and climate are locked in this multiplicative battle, there has to be a mathematical

34:55.800 --> 34:56.720
breaking point, right?

34:56.780 --> 35:01.160
A specific threshold where the ecosystem's defenses stop resisting and the whole thing just violently

35:01.160 --> 35:01.700
gives way.

35:02.020 --> 35:04.040
Does fliction pinpoint where that actually happens?

35:04.560 --> 35:05.020
They do.

35:05.020 --> 35:09.920
And this requires stepping into some highly advanced mathematics regarding decoupling theory.

35:10.880 --> 35:15.640
Flicksheen borrows heavily from harmonic analysis, specifically a recent breakthrough called Fourier

35:15.640 --> 35:18.160
restriction theory and the Burgondimeter theorem.

35:18.300 --> 35:19.320
Okay, lifeline, please.

35:19.460 --> 35:24.200
You are going to have to hold my hand through Fourier restriction theory because right now I

35:24.200 --> 35:28.360
am picturing complex sine waves on a chalkboard and I am completely lost.

35:28.500 --> 35:29.960
I completely understand.

35:30.240 --> 35:31.940
Let's ground it in a physical analogy.

35:32.140 --> 35:32.380
Please?

35:32.380 --> 35:35.840
Imagine throwing two stones into a still pond.

35:36.660 --> 35:38.820
Each stone creates ripples waves.

35:39.260 --> 35:42.840
As those ripples spread out, they eventually crash into each other.

35:42.980 --> 35:43.300
Oh.

35:43.920 --> 35:48.120
Sometimes the peak of one wave hits the trough of another wave and they cancel each other

35:48.120 --> 35:48.380
out.

35:48.780 --> 35:49.680
The water goes flat.

35:50.420 --> 35:51.600
That is a form of independence.

35:51.720 --> 35:51.960
Right.

35:51.960 --> 35:56.480
But sometimes the peak of one wave perfectly aligns with the peak of another wave and they

35:56.480 --> 35:59.220
combine to create a much larger, more powerful wave.

35:59.660 --> 36:01.200
That is called constructive interference.

36:01.520 --> 36:02.740
Okay, I can visualize that.

36:02.960 --> 36:03.740
The waves add up.

36:03.960 --> 36:09.000
Harmonic analysis and the Burgondimeter theorem specifically deals with understanding the exact

36:09.000 --> 36:14.360
mathematical boundaries of when local systems, like those individual ripples, can be treated

36:14.360 --> 36:16.160
as independent events that cancel out.

36:16.160 --> 36:20.320
And when they suddenly violently synchronize into a massive coherent force.

36:20.440 --> 36:21.080
I see.

36:22.120 --> 36:28.040
In the paper, Fliction introduces a metric called the decoupling constant, denoted as D sub pi.

36:28.300 --> 36:33.180
And what does the decoupling constant actually measure in the context of our carbon offset markets?

36:33.180 --> 36:36.100
It measures how badly our assumption of independence fails.

36:36.960 --> 36:38.580
Remember the Wu study from the beginning?

36:38.880 --> 36:42.800
The one that found the California buffer pool was undersized by a factor of 6.3?

36:43.060 --> 36:43.460
